(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-2-physiology-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"Physiology Comprehensive Master Exam — Form A","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"How does the tympanic membrane amplify sound before it reaches the cochlea?","choices":["It generates resonant vibrations of its own that add extra energy independent of incoming sound waves","Its surface area is about 17 times larger than the oval window, concentrating the same force over a smaller area","It has no amplifying effect at all, since the ossicles alone provide all impedance matching","It amplifies only high-frequency sounds above 4000 Hz, leaving low frequencies unchanged"],"answer":1,"exp":"The tympanic membrane transmits vibrations to the cochlea and amplifies the signal because its area is about 17 times larger than the oval window, concentrating force onto the smaller oval window. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is the function of the attenuation reflex, involving the stapedius and tensor tympani muscles?","choices":["A loud noise triggers reflex contraction of both muscles, causing ossicular rigidity that attenuates vibration to the cochlea (reducing transmission by 30-40 decibels), protecting the cochlea","A loud noise triggers relaxation of both muscles, increasing ossicular mobility and amplifying sound transmission by roughly 30 to 40 decibels","It only responds to the high-frequency sounds above 4000 Hz, providing essentially no protective attenuation whatsoever for any lower-frequency noise sources","To amplify soft sounds by relaxing ossicular tension, substantially increasing auditory sensitivity during otherwise quiet listening conditions"],"answer":0,"exp":"The attenuation reflex is triggered by loud noise, causing reflex contraction of the stapedius and tensor tympani muscles, which makes the ossicular system rigid and attenuates vibration transmission to the cochlea by 30-40 decibels, protecting the cochlea and dampening sounds like one's own voice. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is notable about the size of the stapedius muscle?","choices":["It is among the fastest-contracting skeletal muscles in the body","It is entirely absent in roughly 1% of the general human population","It is the smallest skeletal muscle in the body (about 1 mm long)","It is one of the longest skeletal muscles, at nearly 3 cm in length"],"answer":2,"exp":"The stapedius, which attaches to the stapes and participates in the attenuation reflex, is the smallest skeletal muscle in the human body at about 1 mm long. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What are the three coiled tubes (separated by membranes) that make up the cochlea?","choices":["Organ of Corti, tectorial membrane, and basilar membrane","Malleus, incus, and stapes","Cochlear duct, semicircular canal, and utricle","Scala tympani, scala media, and scala vestibuli"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cochlea is a system of three coiled tubes separated by membranes: the scala tympani, scala media, and scala vestibuli; sound waves move the tympanic membrane and stapes, displacing cochlear fluid and inducing vibration in the basilar membrane. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What generates the nerve impulses that encode auditory information, and where is this structure located?","choices":["The tectorial membrane alone, generating impulses through direct mechanical contact with the stapes footplate","Hair cells with stereocilia in the organ of Corti, located on the surface of the basilar membrane, shearing against the overlying tectorial membrane","The oval window, located at the base of the cochlea, via direct piezoelectric stimulation of adjacent hair cells","The round window, located near the apex and close to the helicotrema, through the vibration of its own thin membrane surface"],"answer":1,"exp":"The organ of Corti, lying on the surface of the basilar membrane, contains hair cells with stereocilia; movement of the basilar membrane causes the stereocilia to shear back and forth against the overlying tectorial membrane, generating the nerve impulses. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What percentage of auditory signals are transmitted by inner hair cells, despite there being 3-4x as many outer hair cells?","choices":["100%, with outer hair cells contributing nothing","~50%","~90%","~10%"],"answer":2,"exp":"About 90% of auditory signals are transmitted by inner hair cells, even though there are 3-4 times as many outer hair cells; outer hair cells are thought to control the sensitivity of inner hair cells for different sound pitches. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"How does basilar membrane fiber stiffness change from the base (near the oval window) to the apex (near the helicotrema)?","choices":["Fiber length decreases toward the apex, while stiffness increases roughly 100-fold from base to apex","Stiffness remains constant throughout, with resonant frequency determined solely by fiber length alone","Stiffness decreases about 100-fold toward the apex, with high-frequency resonance near the base and low-frequency resonance near the apex","Stiffness increases about 100-fold toward the apex, with the low-frequency resonance occurring near the base"],"answer":2,"exp":"Basilar membrane fibers increase in length and decrease in diameter from the base (oval window) to the apex (helicotrema), with overall stiffness decreasing about 100-fold — high-frequency resonance occurs near the stiffer base, and low-frequency resonance occurs near the more flexible apex. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the function of the round window in the cochlea?","choices":["It decompresses acoustic energy that enters the cochlea via stapes movement against the oval window","It generates the initial receptor potential via its own dedicated population of hair cells","It is the site where the tectorial membrane attaches to the modiolus of the cochlea","It amplifies incoming sound vibrations before they reach the stapes footplate"],"answer":0,"exp":"The round window serves to decompress (relieve pressure from) the acoustic energy that enters the cochlea via stapes movement against the oval window, allowing fluid within the cochlea to move. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What does the \"place principle\" explain about hearing?","choices":["How the sensations of taste and smell combine centrally to produce the overall perception of flavour","How sound direction is determined, based on intensity and timing differences between the two ears","How the frequency (pitch) of a sound is perceived, based on which position along the basilar membrane is maximally stimulated","How the loudness of a sound is perceived, based on the total number of hair cells activated"],"answer":2,"exp":"The place principle determines the frequency of sound perceived: different sound frequencies cause the basilar membrane to oscillate maximally at different positions, and the position along the membrane where hair cells are stimulated determines the perceived pitch. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What determines the perceived amplitude (loudness) of a sound?","choices":["The number of ossicles involved in transmission","The position along the basilar membrane that is stimulated","How much the basilar membrane is displaced","The size of the tympanic membrane alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"While the place principle (position along the basilar membrane) determines perceived pitch, sound amplitude (loudness) is determined by how much the basilar membrane is displaced. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"How is the decibel unit of sound defined, and why is a logarithmic scale used?","choices":["A decibel represents roughly a 2-fold increase in sound energy; a linear scale is used since the ear's dynamic range is quite limited","Decibels measure the frequency of a sound wave in cycles per second, entirely unrelated to its intensity or energy content","A decibel is a purely linear unit of pressure measured directly in pascals, with no logarithmic scaling involved at all","A 10-fold increase in sound energy equals 1 bel (10 decibels); a logarithmic scale is used because of the extreme range of sound intensities the ear can detect"],"answer":3,"exp":"A 10-fold increase in sound energy equals 1 bel, and 0.1 bel is a decibel (a 1.26-fold increase in energy); sound intensity is expressed logarithmically because of the extreme range of sound intensities encountered, and the ear can barely distinguish a 1-decibel change. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How is the auditory cortex organized, and what happens when it is destroyed?","choices":["Arranged by tonotopic maps (high frequency at one end, low at the other); destruction of the auditory cortex causes loss of discrimination of sound patterns","Organized by loudness maps only, not frequency; destruction eliminates the ability to perceive any sound intensity","Organised somatotopically, much like the sensory cortex; destruction of that area abolishes conscious pitch perception entirely","Destruction only affects the ability to hear high-frequency sounds, leaving low-frequency discrimination fully intact"],"answer":0,"exp":"The auditory cortex and association areas are arranged by tonotopic maps, with high-frequency sounds represented at one end and low-frequency at the other; discrimination of sound patterns is lost when the auditory cortex is destroyed. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why is tetany not possible in cardiac muscle, unlike skeletal muscle?","choices":["Cardiac muscle has a much longer duration of both action potential and contraction compared to skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle contracts too weakly to ever generate a palpable pulse without external pacing assistance","Cardiac muscle cannot generate action potentials at all, relying entirely on mechanical stretch to trigger contraction","Cardiac muscle has no troponin or tropomyosin, relying on a calcium-independent contraction mechanism instead"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cardiac muscle has a longer duration of both action potential and contraction compared to skeletal muscle, which (combined with its long absolute refractory period) prevents tetany from occurring. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What structurally connects individual cardiac muscle fibers into a functional syncytium, and what electrically separates the atrial and ventricular syncytia?","choices":["Tight junctions at intercalated disks connect fibers; loose connective tissue only partially separates the atrial and ventricular syncytia","Gap junctions at intercalated disks connect fibers within a syncytium; a fibrous insulator (skeleton) separates the atrial and ventricular syncytia","Desmosomes alone provide electrical connection between fibers; a bony ring of cartilage separates the two syncytia","The atrial and ventricular syncytia are connected by gap junctions directly through the A-V valve leaflets themselves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Gap junctions at intercalated disks provide low-resistance electrical connections between cardiac muscle fibers, forming atrial and ventricular syncytia; a fibrous insulator (the cardiac skeleton) separates the two syncytia so that impulses must pass through the specialized conduction system. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What two features distinguish the ventricular muscle action potential from the skeletal muscle action potential?","choices":["Skeletal muscle APs show a ~150 ms plateau caused by increased K+ conductance, longer than cardiac muscle's brief 2 ms AP","Cardiac muscle APs show a ~200 ms plateau caused by decreased K+ conductance and increased Ca2+ conductance, versus skeletal muscle's 1-5 ms AP","Both types of muscle have an identical AP duration of about 5 ms, driven purely by fast Na+ channel kinetics","Cardiac muscle APs are shorter than skeletal muscle APs and depend entirely on fast Na+ channels with no calcium involvement"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ventricular muscle action potential shows a roughly 200 ms plateau caused by decreased K+ conductance and increased Ca2+ conductance (an important calcium source for contraction) — features not seen in the skeletal muscle AP, which lasts only 1-5 ms. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"How long does the absolute refractory period last in the ventricles, and what does it prevent?","choices":["0.25-0.30 seconds, preventing tetanus in heart muscle","0.15 seconds, identical to the atrial refractory period","5 seconds, preventing all contraction","0.001 seconds, with no functional significance"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ventricular absolute refractory period lasts about 0.25-0.30 seconds (roughly matching the duration of contraction), preventing the heart muscle from being re-excited and thus preventing tetanus; the atrial refractory period is shorter, about 0.15 seconds. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What triggers ryanodine receptor opening and Ca2+ release from the SR in cardiac muscle EC coupling?","choices":["Ca2+ entering through voltage-gated L-type Ca2+ channels in the T-tubule binds the ryanodine receptor, causing it to open and release a large amount of additional Ca2+ (calcium-activated calcium release)","Direct mechanical deformation of the SR membrane by the T-tubule voltage sensor, physically coupled to the ryanodine receptor without any calcium involvement","ATP binding directly to the ryanodine receptor following its release from nearby mitochondria during periods of sustained high-frequency cardiac stimulation","Voltage alone acting directly on the ryanodine receptor through a mechanical protein linkage, with no calcium involvement at all, exactly as in skeletal muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"In cardiac EC coupling, an action potential opens voltage-gated L-type Ca2+ channels in the T-tubule, allowing Ca2+ to enter the sarcoplasm; this Ca2+ binds to the ryanodine receptor, causing it to open and release a much larger amount of Ca2+ from the SR — calcium-activated calcium release (CACR). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"How do cardiac T-tubules compare to skeletal muscle T-tubules in volume, and what is the functional consequence?","choices":["Cardiac T-tubules are about 25 times smaller than skeletal muscle T-tubules, and cardiac contraction strength depends only on intracellular SR calcium stores","Cardiac T-tubules have about 25x greater volume than skeletal muscle T-tubules, and (unlike skeletal muscle) cardiac contraction strength depends on extracellular calcium concentration","T-tubule volume is identical between the two muscle types, and both rely equally heavily on extracellular calcium for contraction strength","Extracellular calcium has no effect on cardiac contraction strength, which depends entirely on calcium stored within the sarcoplasmic reticulum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cardiac T-tubules have about 25 times greater volume than skeletal muscle T-tubules and store calcium (bound to negatively charged mucopolysaccharides); because cardiac muscle relies on extracellular calcium entering via the T-tubule (not just SR release, as in skeletal muscle), the strength of ventricular contraction depends on extracellular calcium concentration. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What are the two main phases of systole and the four sub-phases of diastole in the cardiac cycle?","choices":["Systole: isovolumic contraction and ejection; diastole: isovolumic relaxation, rapid inflow, diastasis, and atrial systole","Systole has only one phase, ejection, lasting the entire 0.3 seconds of ventricular contraction each cycle","Systole: rapid inflow and diastasis; diastole: isovolumic contraction, ejection, and atrial systole","There are no distinct phases within systole or diastole, since ventricular pressure changes continuously and smoothly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Systole consists of isovolumic contraction and ejection; diastole consists of isovolumic relaxation, rapid inflow, diastasis, and atrial systole. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What causes the first and second heart sounds, and which valve types are responsible for each?","choices":["Heart sounds are unrelated to valve closure, arising instead from turbulent blood flow through partially open valves","S1 (soft, \"lub\") is caused by closure of the semilunar valves; S2 (rapid snap, \"dub\") is caused by closure of the A-V valves","Both heart sounds are caused by simultaneous closure of all four cardiac valves at the same instant","S1 (soft, \"lub\") is caused by closure of the A-V valves; S2 (rapid snap, \"dub\") is caused by closure of the semilunar valves"],"answer":3,"exp":"The first heart sound (S1, soft closure, \"lub\") is caused by closure of the A-V valves (mitral and tricuspid); the second heart sound (S2, rapid snap, \"dub\") is caused by closure of the semilunar valves (pulmonary and aortic). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Do papillary muscles help close the heart valves?","choices":["Papillary muscles have no relationship to the cardiac valves at all, functioning only to anchor the ventricular walls during contraction","They only function during diastole, relaxing to allow the A-V valve leaflets to open fully for ventricular filling","Yes, they are the primary mechanism of valve closure, contracting forcefully just before systole to pull the leaflets fully shut","No — they contract when the ventricles contract, but their role is to prevent the valve vanes from bulging back into the atria, not to help the valves close"],"answer":3,"exp":"Papillary muscles contract when the ventricles contract, but they do not help the valves close — their function is to prevent the AV valve vanes (via chordae tendineae) from bulging backward into the atria during ventricular contraction. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How is ejection fraction (EF) calculated, and what does it clinically assess?","choices":["EF = EDV / SV (end-diastolic volume divided by stroke volume), used to estimate total blood volume","EF = ESV / EDV (end-systolic volume divided by end-diastolic volume), used clinically to estimate heart rate","EF has no clinical relevance, since it varies too unpredictably between healthy individuals to be diagnostically useful","EF = SV / EDV (stroke volume divided by end-diastolic volume), used clinically to assess cardiac pumping capability"],"answer":3,"exp":"Ejection fraction (EF) = stroke volume (SV) / end-diastolic volume (EDV); stroke volume itself equals EDV minus end-systolic volume (ESV). EF is used clinically (often measured by echocardiogram) to assess cardiac pumping capability. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What causes the incisura (dicrotic notch) seen on the aortic pressure curve, and when is it absent?","choices":["It has no relationship to valve function, arising instead purely from turbulent blood flow circulating within the aortic arch","It is caused by atrial contraction pushing blood forward into the ventricle, and it is absent whenever atrial fibrillation is present","It is caused by a brief period of backward flow just before aortic valve closure followed by sudden cessation of backflow; it is absent with aortic regurgitation","It reflects mitral valve opening at the start of diastole, and is characteristically absent in cases of severe mitral stenosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The incisura (dicrotic notch) occurs with aortic valve closure, caused by a short period of backward flow immediately before valve closure followed by sudden cessation of that backflow; it is absent in aortic regurgitation, where the valve doesn't close properly. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What causes the \"c wave\" seen in the atrial pressure curve?","choices":["Slow, continuous venous return filling the atria during ventricular systole","Atrial contraction itself, pushing the a wave upward just before the c wave","Contracting ventricles causing the A-V valves to bulge backward toward the atria","The rapid opening of the semilunar valves at the start of ventricular ejection"],"answer":2,"exp":"The atrial pressure curve's \"c wave\" is caused by contracting ventricles pushing the A-V valves to bulge backward toward the atria; the \"a wave\" reflects atrial contraction, and the \"v wave\" reflects slow atrial filling followed by rapid ventricular filling once the A-V valves open. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is the primary functional role of arterioles in the circulation?","choices":["Low-pressure reservoir holding the majority of total circulating blood volume","Control site for blood flow and the major resistance site of the circulation","Primary site of water and solute exchange between blood and interstitial fluid","Primary site of gas exchange between alveolar air and pulmonary capillary blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Arterioles are the control site for blood flow and the major resistance site of the circulation, in contrast to capillaries (exchange site) and large veins/venules (low-pressure reservoir). — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is the primary functional role of capillaries?","choices":["The primary site controlling total peripheral vascular resistance","The major low-pressure reservoir holding most of the circulating blood","The major site of water and solute exchange between blood and tissues","The primary conduit delivering oxygenated blood at high systolic pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Capillaries are the major site of water and solute exchange between blood and tissues, in contrast to the aorta/large arteries (high-pressure transport) and arterioles (resistance control). — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which vascular component has by far the largest total cross-sectional area, and what does this mean for blood velocity there?","choices":["The aorta (about 2.5 cm² in cross-sectional area), with the fastest overall blood velocity","The capillaries (about 1000x the aorta's cross-sectional area), with the slowest blood velocity","The venae cavae (about 8 cm² combined), with a moderately fast venous return velocity","All vascular components have roughly equal cross-sectional area throughout the entire circulatory tree"],"answer":1,"exp":"Capillaries have by far the largest total cross-sectional area (about 2500 cm², roughly 1000 times that of the aorta at 2.5 cm²); since velocity = flow/cross-sectional area, blood velocity is slowest in the capillaries, allowing time for exchange. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Where is the majority of total blood volume located within the circulation?","choices":["In the veins (about 60%), which serve as a blood reservoir","In the arteries (about 15%), which serve as a pressure conduit","In the capillaries (about 5%), where exchange primarily occurs","In the pulmonary circulation (about 60%), acting as a reservoir"],"answer":0,"exp":"The majority of blood volume (about 60%) is located in the veins, which serve as a blood reservoir, with total blood volume in humans being about 5 liters. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"How does blood pressure change across the circulatory system, and where does the largest pressure drop occur?","choices":["Pressure is high in the arterial tree and low on the venous side, with the largest pressure drop occurring across the arteriolar-capillary junction","Pressure is constant throughout the entire circulation, since vascular resistance is distributed evenly across every vessel type","The largest pressure drop occurs across the venae cavae themselves, just before blood returns to the right atrium","Pressure is highest in the veins and lowest in the arteries, reversing the normal direction of blood flow throughout"],"answer":0,"exp":"Blood pressure is high in the arterial tree and progressively lower on the venous side, with the largest single pressure drop occurring across the arteriolar-capillary junction — reflecting the arterioles' role as the major resistance site. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"According to the basic theory of circulatory function, what mainly controls cardiac output, and is arterial pressure controlled by the same mechanism?","choices":["Arterial pressure and cardiac output are controlled by an identical single mechanism, centered entirely on carotid and aortic baroreceptor reflexes","Cardiac output has no relationship to tissue needs whatsoever, being fixed entirely by the intrinsic SA node firing rate alone","Cardiac output is mainly controlled by local tissue blood flow needs; arterial pressure is controlled independently of local flow or cardiac output control","Local tissue flow has no bearing on cardiac output at all, which is instead set entirely by central venous pressure alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Blood flow to tissues is controlled in relation to tissue needs, and cardiac output is mainly controlled by local tissue flow; arterial pressure, by contrast, is controlled independently of either local blood flow control or cardiac output control. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"According to Q = ΔP/R, what two factors determine blood flow through a vessel?","choices":["Blood flow is independent of pressure and resistance, determined only by total blood volume","Only the diameter of the heart's chambers, unrelated to any pressure gradient along the vessel","The pressure difference (ΔP) between the two ends of the vessel and the resistance (R) of the vessel","Only vessel length and blood viscosity, with no contribution from the pressure gradient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Flow (Q) through a blood vessel is determined by the pressure difference (ΔP, or P1 − P2) between the two ends of the vessel and the resistance (R) of the vessel, following Q = ΔP/R. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is laminar flow, and what velocity profile does it create?","choices":["Laminar flow creates a completely uniform velocity profile across the entire vessel, with no difference between center and wall","Laminar flow only occurs in veins, never in arteries, due to the much lower blood pressures found on the venous side of circulation","Blood flows randomly with no streamline pattern at all, creating a flat velocity profile identical at both the center and near the wall","Blood flows in streamlines with each layer remaining the same distance from the wall, creating a parabolic profile where central blood velocity is greater than near the wall"],"answer":3,"exp":"Laminar flow occurs when blood flows in streamlines, with each layer remaining the same distance from the vessel wall; this creates a parabolic velocity profile where blood in the center of the vessel moves faster than blood near the outer edge. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How is vascular resistance calculated?","choices":["R = ∆P/Q (pressure difference divided by blood flow)","R = Q/∆P","R = ∆P × Q","Resistance cannot be calculated from pressure and flow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Resistance (R) is calculated by dividing the pressure difference (ΔP) between two points in a vessel by the vessel's blood flow (Q): R = ΔP/Q. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"In a parallel arrangement of vascular resistances (as in the peripheral circulation), how does total resistance compare to the resistance of the single lowest-resistance vessel?","choices":["Total resistance is less than the resistance of the vessel with the lowest resistance","Total resistance equals the sum of all individual resistances added together in sequence","Parallel arrangement has no measurable effect on total resistance compared to a single vessel","Total resistance is always exactly equal to the average of all individual vessel resistances"],"answer":0,"exp":"In a parallel arrangement (as in the peripheral circulation, allowing independent control of flow to different tissues), total resistance is actually less than the resistance of the single vessel with the lowest resistance. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How does vascular conductance change with vessel radius, according to Poiseuille's law?","choices":["Conductance is inversely proportional to the square of the radius","Conductance increases in proportion to the fourth power of the radius","Conductance increases in proportion to the square of the radius","Conductance is independent of radius, depending only on vessel length"],"answer":1,"exp":"Vascular conductance is very sensitive to changes in vessel diameter — it increases in proportion to the fourth power of the radius (Q = πΔPr⁴/8ηL), meaning small changes in radius produce large changes in flow. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"If a vessel narrows to half its original radius, by approximately how much does resistance increase?","choices":["No change in resistance","4-fold","16-fold","2-fold"],"answer":2,"exp":"Because resistance is inversely proportional to the fourth power of the radius, a vessel that narrows by half increases resistance approximately 16-fold. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"How do the kidneys play a dominant role in long-term arterial pressure control?","choices":["The kidneys have no relationship to long-term pressure, which is instead set entirely by baroreceptor resetting over weeks","As ECFV increases, arterial pressure increases; this rise in pressure causes the kidneys to excrete Na and water, returning ECFV to normal","Increased ECFV always decreases arterial pressure by triggering compensatory vasodilation throughout the splanchnic circulation","The kidneys only affect short-term, second-to-second pressure through rapid renin release triggered by baroreceptors"],"answer":1,"exp":"As extracellular fluid volume (ECFV) increases, arterial pressure increases; this pressure increase causes the kidneys to excrete more sodium and water (returning ECFV to normal) — a negative feedback loop that dominates long-term pressure control. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is the difference between pressure diuresis and pressure natriuresis?","choices":["Pressure diuresis is the effect of pressure to increase water excretion; pressure natriuresis is the effect of pressure to increase Na excretion","They are identical terms describing the kidney's response to falling blood glucose rather than arterial pressure","Pressure diuresis decreases Na excretion; pressure natriuresis decreases water excretion, both in response to falling pressure","Neither phenomenon relates to arterial pressure; both instead describe responses purely to changes in plasma osmolarity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pressure diuresis is the effect of increased arterial pressure to increase water excretion by the kidneys, while pressure natriuresis is the effect of increased pressure to increase sodium excretion — both underlie the kidney's central role in long-term pressure regulation. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"In the graphic analysis of the renal-body fluid mechanism, what defines the equilibrium point for long-term arterial pressure?","choices":["The point where cardiac output exactly equals total peripheral resistance","The point at which aldosterone secretion reaches its maximal physiological rate","The point where the renal function curve (output) intersects the salt and water intake line","The point where mean systemic filling pressure equals right atrial pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"The equilibrium point for long-term arterial pressure is where the renal function (output) curve intersects the salt and water intake line; the renal-body fluid feedback system is described as having an \"infinite gain\" for long-term pressure control. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Do long-term changes in total peripheral resistance (TPR) alone raise long-term arterial pressure?","choices":["Long-term pressure is entirely independent of the kidneys, being determined solely by baseline sympathetic vasoconstrictor tone alone","TPR changes only affect short-term pressure within seconds, while renal mechanisms determine every long-term pressure value alone","Yes — any sustained rise in TPR, regardless of which vascular bed happens to be affected, directly and permanently raises the long-term arterial pressure","No — changes in TPR do not affect the long-term arterial pressure level unless the renal function curve itself is altered (e.g., by changing renal vascular resistance)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Changes in total peripheral resistance (TPR) do not affect the long-term level of arterial pressure unless they alter the renal function curve — for example, changing renal vascular resistance specifically does produce long-term pressure changes, but changing resistance elsewhere in the body does not. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Where is renin synthesized and stored, and what stimulates its release?","choices":["In the zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex, released in response to an elevated circulating blood volume","In the posterior pituitary gland, released in response to increased plasma osmolarity","In modified smooth muscle cells of the afferent arterioles of the kidney, released in response to a fall in pressure","In hepatocytes of the liver, released in response to increased dietary sodium intake"],"answer":2,"exp":"Renin is synthesized and stored in modified smooth muscle cells in the afferent arterioles of the kidney, and is released in response to a fall in blood pressure. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Where does angiotensin I get converted to angiotensin II?","choices":["In the liver, by an enzyme secreted from hepatocyte cell membranes","In the adrenal medulla, by an enzyme co-released with epinephrine","By a converting enzyme located in endothelial cells of the pulmonary circulation","In the kidney nephron, by an enzyme secreted from the proximal tubule"],"answer":2,"exp":"Renin acts on angiotensinogen to form angiotensin I (ANG I), which is then converted to angiotensin II (ANG II) by a converting enzyme (ACE) located in the endothelial cells of the pulmonary circulation. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What are the three major actions of the renin-angiotensin system on arterial pressure regulation?","choices":["Only mild vasodilation of the coronary and cerebral circulations, with no direct renal effects","Vasodilation, increased sodium excretion, and shifting the renal function curve leftward toward lower pressures","Vasoconstriction, Na retention (via direct and indirect renal actions), and shifting the renal function curve to the right","Decreasing sympathetic activity while simultaneously suppressing aldosterone secretion from the adrenal cortex"],"answer":2,"exp":"The renin-angiotensin system causes vasoconstriction, causes sodium retention by direct and indirect actions on the kidney, and causes a rightward shift in the renal function curve — all of which help buffer drops in arterial pressure (e.g., during hemorrhage). — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which of the following factors decreases renal excretory function and increases blood pressure?","choices":["Dopamine, prostaglandins, bradykinin, and atrial natriuretic peptide","Nitric oxide, prostaglandins, dopamine, and atrial natriuretic peptide","Atrial natriuretic peptide, dopamine, nitric oxide, and bradykinin","Angiotensin II, aldosterone, sympathetic nervous activity, and endothelin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Angiotensin II, aldosterone, sympathetic nervous activity, and endothelin all decrease renal excretory function and thereby increase blood pressure, in contrast to atrial natriuretic peptide, nitric oxide, and dopamine, which increase renal excretion and reduce blood pressure. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the fundamental relationship between venous return and cardiac output in a steady state?","choices":["Venous return is always greater than cardiac output","Cardiac output is always double venous return","They are unrelated quantities","Venous return and cardiac output must be equal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Venous return (blood flowing from the veins into the right atrium each minute) and cardiac output (blood pumped into the aorta each minute) must be equal, since the heart cannot pump more blood than it receives, and cannot indefinitely accumulate excess blood. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the most important factor directly affecting cardiac output, according to this lecture?","choices":["Age","The basic level of body metabolism","Only physical activity, with metabolism playing no role","Body size alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"The basic level of body metabolism is listed as the most important direct factor affecting cardiac output, followed by physical activity, age, and body size. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What does the Frank-Starling law of the heart state?","choices":["Increased blood flow into the heart decreases contraction force by overstretching the sarcomeres beyond optimal length","Only the atria, never the ventricles, contract with increased force in response to stretch","Increased blood flow into the heart stretches the chamber walls, causing the cardiac muscle to contract with increased force","The heart's contraction force depends solely on sympathetic stimulation, independent of chamber filling volume"],"answer":2,"exp":"The Frank-Starling law of the heart states that increased quantities of blood flowing into the heart stretch the walls of the heart chambers, causing cardiac muscle to contract with increased force — the mechanistic basis of the Frank-Starling mechanism/cardiac function curve. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the Bainbridge reflex, and what triggers it?","choices":["A reflex triggered by stretch of the left atrium that signals the vasomotor center to gradually decrease heart rate and contractility","A reflex exclusive to the renal afferent arterioles that triggers compensatory renin release during acute hypotension","A nervous reflex triggered by stretch of the right atrium, passing to the vasomotor center and back via sympathetic/vagal nerves, that increases heart rate","A reflex that only decreases contractility via vagal fibers, with no measurable effect on sinus node firing rate at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"The Bainbridge reflex is initiated by stretch of the right atrium (e.g., from increased venous return), which sends signals to the vasomotor center in the brain, which then signals back to the heart via sympathetic and vagal nerves to increase heart rate — preventing blood from \"damming up\" in the veins/atria. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How is the refractive index of a substance defined?","choices":["The ratio of the speed of light in air to the speed of light in the substance","The ratio of a substance's surface curvature to its overall focal length","The wavelength of light that a substance absorbs most strongly","The ratio of a substance's density to the density of water"],"answer":0,"exp":"The refractive index is the ratio of the speed of light in air to its speed in the substance; for example, if light travels at 200,000 km/sec in a substance (versus 300,000 km/sec in air), the refractive index is 300,000/200,000 = 1.5. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is the difference between how convex and concave lenses affect light rays?","choices":["A convex lens focuses light rays; a concave lens diverges light rays","Both lens types focus light equally, differing only in focal distance","A convex lens inverts the image; a concave lens leaves it upright","Both lens types diverge light rays, differing only in the degree"],"answer":0,"exp":"A convex lens focuses light rays to a point, while a concave lens diverges light rays outward. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What fraction of the eye's total refractive power comes from the anterior surface of the cornea, and why does vision blur underwater?","choices":["About 2/3, and this refraction is virtually eliminated underwater because water has a refractive index close to that of the cornea","The cornea contributes no refractive power at all, with the lens alone responsible for underwater blurring","All of the refractive power, and vision stays sharp underwater since the lens compensates fully for water's index","About 1/3, and underwater blurring is caused entirely by the lens losing its accommodative ability"],"answer":0,"exp":"About two-thirds of the eye's refractive power comes from the anterior surface of the cornea; underwater, this refraction is virtually eliminated because water's refractive index is close to that of the cornea, producing a blurry image. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is a diopter, and what is the total refractive power of a normal eye?","choices":["A diopter has no single fixed definition; the eye's total refractive power is said to vary quite unpredictably from about 40 to 80 diopters depending on the ambient lighting conditions","A diopter measures intraocular pressure directly in millimeters of mercury; the total refractive power of a healthy adult eye is normally about 15 to 20 mm Hg","A diopter is the power of a lens that focuses parallel light rays at 1 meter; the eye's total refractive power is about 59 diopters (since the retina sits 17mm behind the refractive center, 1000/17 ≈ 59)","A diopter is a unit that measures visual acuity on the Snellen chart scale; the eye's total refractive power is conventionally expressed simply as 20/20 at rest"],"answer":2,"exp":"A diopter is a measure of lens power — 1 diopter is the ability to focus parallel light rays at a distance of 1 meter; since the retina sits about 17 mm behind the eye's refractive center, the eye's total refractive power is about 59 diopters (1000/17). — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What happens to the lens during accommodation, and what nervous system controls this process?","choices":["Contraction of the ciliary muscle relaxes the suspensory ligaments, allowing the lens to become thicker (increasing refractive power from 20 to as much as 34 diopters); controlled by the parasympathetic nervous system","Accommodation is controlled entirely by the somatic motor system acting directly on the extraocular muscles, with no autonomic nervous system involvement whatsoever","Accommodation involves no change in lens shape at all; instead, the entire eyeball is thought to elongate slightly to bring near objects into sharper focus each time","Contraction of the ciliary muscle tightens the suspensory ligaments, making the lens flatter and considerably less powerful; this whole process is controlled by the sympathetic nervous system"],"answer":0,"exp":"During accommodation, contraction of the ciliary muscle (attached to the suspensory/zonule ligaments) relaxes tension on the lens, allowing it to become thicker and more spherical, increasing its refractive power from about 20 to as much as 34 diopters; this process is under parasympathetic control. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is presbyopia, and what causes it?","choices":["Nearsightedness caused by an abnormally elongated eyeball shape, present since birth","The age-related inability to accommodate, caused by denaturation of lens proteins that makes the lens less elastic","A bacterial infection of the cornea that gradually clouds and scars the visual axis","A disorder of the retina causing the progressive loss of both colour vision and the peripheral visual fields"],"answer":1,"exp":"Presbyopia is the inability to accommodate, caused by denaturation of proteins within the lens that makes it progressively less elastic with age — accommodation power decreases from about 14 diopters in childhood to 2 diopters by age 50 and to essentially 0 by age 70. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What does 20/40 vision mean?","choices":["What a normal person can see at 40 feet, this person must be at 20 feet to see — indicating below-normal acuity","What a normal person can see at 20 feet, this person can see just as easily from 40 feet — indicating above-normal acuity","What a normal person can see at 20 feet, someone with this notation needs 40 feet to see equally clearly — indicating impaired acuity","A ratio comparing pupil diameter to retinal distance, used specifically to grade the severity of astigmatism rather than acuity"],"answer":0,"exp":"20/40 vision means that what a normal person can see clearly at 40 feet, this person must be at 20 feet to see — indicating worse than normal (20/20) visual acuity. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Where is aqueous humor produced, and what is its general flow pathway out of the eye?","choices":["Produced by the choroid; flows posteriorly into the vitreous chamber, then diffuses through the retina into the optic nerve sheath","Produced by the lens epithelium; flows anteriorly into the posterior chamber, then exits directly through the sclera via emissary veins","Produced by the ciliary body; flows through the pupil into the anterior chamber, through the trabecular meshwork into the canal of Schlemm, then into aqueous and extraocular veins","Produced by the cornea's own endothelium; it flows through the anterior chamber and drains directly into the episcleral veins, bypassing the trabecular meshwork entirely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Aqueous humor is produced by the ciliary body (at a rate of about 2-3 microliters/min) and flows through the pupil into the anterior chamber, through the trabecular meshwork, into the canal of Schlemm, and then into aqueous veins and extraocular veins. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is normal intraocular pressure, and what determines its level?","choices":["Normally about 8 mm Hg in infants, determined mainly by the rate of aqueous humor secretion from the ciliary processes rather than outflow","Normally about 30 mm Hg in older adults, determined by the elastic recoil of the sclera combined with fluctuating choroidal vascular pressure","Normally about 10 mm Hg, determined entirely by the osmotic pressure gradient created by vitreous humor production within the posterior chamber","Normally 15 mm Hg, determined by resistance to aqueous humor outflow through the canal of Schlemm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Normal intraocular pressure is about 15 mm Hg (range 2-20 mm Hg), determined by the resistance to outflow of aqueous humor at the canal of Schlemm; chronically elevated pressure is a risk factor for glaucoma and can damage the optic nerve. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Approximately how many rods, cones, and ganglion cells does each human retina contain?","choices":["10,000 rods, 10,000 cones, and 10,000 ganglion cells","100 million rods, 3 million cones, and 1.6 million ganglion cells","3 million rods, 100 million cones, and 1.6 million ganglion cells","Equal numbers (1 million each) of rods, cones, and ganglion cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Each retina contains approximately 100 million rods, 3 million cones, and 1.6 million ganglion cells. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is special about the central fovea (fovea centralis) that gives it the highest visual acuity?","choices":["It has the thickest neural layer of any region of the retina, with ganglion and bipolar cells stacked directly on top of the photoreceptors","It contains a mix of rods and cones at roughly a 1:1 ratio, with blood vessels running directly over the photoreceptor layer","It contains only cones, with a 1:1 ratio of cones to bipolar cells, and neurons/vessels are displaced to the sides so light strikes cones with minimal obstruction","It has no photoreceptors at all in this region, relying entirely on surrounding parafoveal rods to relay visual information"],"answer":2,"exp":"The central fovea contains only cones (no rods), with a 1:1 ratio of cones to bipolar cells; neuronal cells and blood vessels are displaced to the sides so light can strike the cones with minimal obstruction — together explaining why the fovea has the greatest visual acuity in the retina. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How do rods and cones differ in light sensitivity and photopigment content?","choices":["Rods and cones both saturate at the same light intensity, with photopigment content scaling directly with retinal eccentricity","Rods have high sensitivity (specialized for night/scotopic vision) with more photopigment and high amplification; cones have lower sensitivity (specialized for day/photopic vision) with less photopigment","Rods saturate only in very intense light and contain less photopigment than cones, making them specialized for bright daylight vision","Cones are considerably more sensitive than rods in dim light, containing far more photopigment and showing much greater amplification for reliable single-photon detection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Rods have high sensitivity (specialized for night/scotopic vision), contain more photopigment, and show high amplification capable of single-photon detection, but saturate in daylight; cones have lower sensitivity (specialized for day/photopic vision), less photopigment, and saturate only with intense light. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 18"}]};
